WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Marcus hears that life insurance is tax-free and assumes every transaction

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.460
receives the same treatment.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.140
Start by identifying the event.

4
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.380
Growth inside a qualifying life policy generally is not taxed to

5
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.540
the owner each year merely because cash value increased.

6
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.260
Personal life insurance premiums generally are paid with after-tax money and

7
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.380
are not deductible. Taking a distribution, surrendering the policy, receiving

8
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.840
a death benefit and calculating an estate are different events with

9
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.540
different rules. This lesson uses personally owned coverage meeting the

10
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.360
federal life insurance definition.

11
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.220
Modified endowment contracts receive a separate lesson because their

12
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.580
distribution rules differ. Never replace those distinctions with a promise

13
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.160
that all money is tax-free.

14
00:00:48.786 --> 00:00:53.526
A participating policy dividend generally represents a return of premium for

15
00:00:53.526 --> 00:00:57.346
federal income-tax purposes, to the extent it does not exceed the

16
00:00:57.346 --> 00:00:59.446
owner's remaining investment in the contract.

17
00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:02.546
That investment is often called basis.

18
00:01:03.466 --> 00:01:08.126
Prior tax-free returns can reduce basis, so use the adjusted figure

19
00:01:08.126 --> 00:01:11.166
rather than blindly adding every historical premium.

20
00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:15.466
If Marcus leaves dividends with the insurer to earn interest, the

21
00:01:15.466 --> 00:01:19.326
added interest generally is taxable even when the underlying dividend is

22
00:01:19.326 --> 00:01:21.226
a nontaxable premium return.

23
00:01:22.166 --> 00:01:26.806
Assume a stated eight hundred dollar dividend is entirely within unrecovered

24
00:01:26.806 --> 00:01:30.306
premiums and separately earns forty dollars of interest.

25
00:01:31.166 --> 00:01:34.446
The forty dollars is interest income under these assumptions.

26
00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.660
For an in-force nonmodified life contract, an ordinary policy loan generally

27
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.480
is indebtedness rather than current taxable income.

28
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.100
Interest and policy debt still matter.

29
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.200
A withdrawal generally recovers basis before gain under the ordinary rule,

30
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.120
but special rules can apply, including certain early benefit reductions.

31
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.860
Do not assume every withdrawal receives identical treatment.

32
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.940
If the policy later lapses or is surrendered with a loan

33
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.800
outstanding, discharge of that debt can count toward the taxable

34
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.860
distribution. Income can arise even when little or no new cash

35
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.920
reaches Marcus. Keeping the policy in force and monitoring debt is

36
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.400
therefore different from simply calling the original loan tax-free forever.

37
00:02:23.314 --> 00:02:28.201
Assume Marcus fully surrenders a policy and receives fifty-five thousand

38
00:02:28.201 --> 00:02:33.334
dollars. His adjusted investment in the contract is forty thousand dollars,

39
00:02:33.334 --> 00:02:38.454
and the example has no outstanding loan, other payment or further

40
00:02:38.454 --> 00:02:43.174
adjustment. The fifteen thousand dollar excess is ordinary income.

41
00:02:43.954 --> 00:02:48.334
The forty thousand dollars of recovered basis is not taxed again.

42
00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:52.814
This is not a death benefit, and keeping the policy for

43
00:02:52.814 --> 00:02:56.634
many years does not turn its surrender gain into a capital

44
00:02:56.634 --> 00:03:01.134
gain. In a different case with policy debt, the amount treated

45
00:03:01.134 --> 00:03:05.414
as received can include money used to discharge the loan, so

46
00:03:05.414 --> 00:03:09.074
the cash check alone may not show the entire taxable event.

47
00:03:09.648 --> 00:03:14.208
Elena receives a qualifying two hundred thousand dollar death benefit plus

48
00:03:14.208 --> 00:03:16.748
three thousand dollars of separately stated interest.

49
00:03:17.308 --> 00:03:22.008
Assuming no exception to the ordinary death-benefit exclusion, the benefit is

50
00:03:22.008 --> 00:03:25.608
excluded from federal gross income and the interest is taxable.

51
00:03:26.508 --> 00:03:29.868
Choosing installments does not make all later interest disappear.

52
00:03:30.488 --> 00:03:34.248
Suppose the insurer holds a two hundred thousand dollar benefit for

53
00:03:34.248 --> 00:03:37.288
one hundred installments of twenty-four hundred dollars each.

54
00:03:37.708 --> 00:03:42.168
Under the stated fixed-installment calculation, two thousand dollars of each

55
00:03:42.168 --> 00:03:46.048
payment recovers the benefit, and four hundred dollars is interest.

56
00:03:46.988 --> 00:03:49.708
Life-contingent installments use additional calculations.

57
00:03:50.868 --> 00:03:55.308
Also check transfer-for-value and employer-owned coverage rules before

58
00:03:55.308 --> 00:03:58.848
assuming every death payment qualifies for the full exclusion.

59
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.950
Now ask a different question: whether insurance is included in Marcus's

60
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.030
federal gross estate. Proceeds payable to his estate are generally included.

61
00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.890
Proceeds payable to another beneficiary can also be included if he

62
00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.570
retained incidents of ownership at death, such as the power to

63
00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:18.290
change beneficiaries, surrender or borrow against the policy.

64
00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:22.530
Certain transfers within three years of death can also bring proceeds

65
00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.910
into the estate. Direct payment to Elena can avoid ordinary probate

66
00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.350
administration while still requiring an estate-tax analysis.

67
00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.170
Inclusion does not automatically mean tax is owed, because deductions and

68
00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.050
applicable exclusions also matter.

69
00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:42.250
Keep the recipient, income-tax result, probate treatment and estate inclusion

70
00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.130
as four separate questions.
